WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
10 years ago I wrote a book called Buy

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:05.680
Buttons. The premise was this, the

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.760
hardest part of any business isn't

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.040
making the thing, it's finding somebody

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.920
to buy the thing. So, instead [music] of

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:15.160
building a store, a website, a product,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.320
a service and hoping somebody finds it,

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.120
the strategy was instead to go set up

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:22.520
shop where the traffic already is, where

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:25.760
the buyers are already buying. We talked

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.280
about Amazon, eBay, Etsy, Fiverr, Udemy,

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.640
the App Store. The book [music] featured

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.680
over 300 of these different apps and

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.680
marketplaces. Places where you could put

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.200
your buy button in whatever niche you

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.800
were in and connect with customers. Put

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:41.000
it in another way, the best fisherman

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.320
fish where there's fish. The book was

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.360
about where to find those [music]

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.560
metaphorical fish, but at a broader

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.160
level it was about short-cutting your

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.600
path to profitability by going where the

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.880
cash was already flowing. A decade

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.480
later, does it still hold up? Are those

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.680
platforms tapped out? What new ones

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.440
showed up? And could the same basic

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.360
marketing principle work when you take

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.960
it offline? That's what we're covering

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.120
today and first up are the legacy

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.120
platforms, the ones that if they weren't

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.880
household names 10 years ago, they

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.840
probably are today.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.680
When we covered Etsy in the book, it was

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.840
in the context of selling handmade

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.720
physical items and that's still a part

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.280
of Etsy's business, but I'm way more

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.080
attracted to the idea of being able to

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.920
create something once and sell it to

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.800
unlimited customers, not just a one-off

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:32.120
order. Which brings me to printables and

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.200
digital products. Is it crowded? Yes,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.120
but you got to remember the buyer pool

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.040
has gotten a lot bigger too and new

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.160
product trends happen all the time. I

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.760
sat down with Cody Berman who's been

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.200
